1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,084
Right now, as you watch this, Earth has two moons.

2
00:00:03,584 --> 00:00:05,081
One is the Moon you know.

3
00:00:05,081 --> 00:00:08,912
The other is a tiny rock, caught by Earth's gravity this spring,

4
00:00:08,912 --> 00:00:11,247
and it's looping around us as we speak.

5
00:00:11,747 --> 00:00:14,086
So what if that second moon wasn't tiny?

6
00:00:14,086 --> 00:00:15,957
What if Earth had two big moons?

7
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,300
First, what actually happened.

8
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:28,112
On August 20, Hungarian astronomer Krisztián Sárneczky spotted a faint, moving dot.

9
00:00:28,112 --> 00:00:33,223
At first, it wasn't even clear if it was a rock or a piece of space junk.

10
00:00:33,723 --> 00:00:36,441
Follow-up observations settled it.

11
00:00:36,441 --> 00:00:37,560
On October 29,

12
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:44,433
the International Astronomical Union's Minor Planet Center announced a natural object,

13
00:00:44,433 --> 00:00:46,671
most likely a tiny asteroid.

14
00:00:46,671 --> 00:00:48,350
Its name is 2026 QQ8.

15
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:50,468
Tracing its path backwards,

16
00:00:50,468 --> 00:00:54,664
astronomers found it had been caught by Earth's gravity back in April.

17
00:00:54,664 --> 00:00:56,402
Its path isn't a neat circle.

18
00:00:56,402 --> 00:00:58,259
It makes messy loops around us,

19
00:00:58,259 --> 00:01:03,234
and never comes closer than about five hundred and sixty-eight thousand kilometers.

20
00:01:03,234 --> 00:01:06,770
That's about one and a half times the distance to the Moon.

21
00:01:07,270 --> 00:01:08,679
What's still unknown?

22
00:01:08,679 --> 00:01:09,686
Its exact size.

23
00:01:09,686 --> 00:01:13,176
Estimates range from about one to two meters across.

24
00:01:13,176 --> 00:01:14,787
And its exact exit date.

25
00:01:14,787 --> 00:01:17,807
It's expected to stay bound through December,

26
00:01:17,807 --> 00:01:21,163
then drift back into its own orbit around the Sun,

27
00:01:21,163 --> 00:01:22,841
probably early next year.

28
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:25,306
And here's the strange part.

29
00:01:25,306 --> 00:01:26,797
This happens all the time.

30
00:01:27,297 --> 00:01:33,151
Small rocks that circle the Sun on paths very like Earth's sometimes drift slowly past us,

31
00:01:33,151 --> 00:01:34,908
and get caught for a while.

32
00:01:34,908 --> 00:01:40,306
One estimate suggests Earth could have as many as six mini-moons at any given time.

33
00:01:40,306 --> 00:01:42,388
Most are just too small to spot.

34
00:01:42,888 --> 00:01:44,493
We've caught a few.

35
00:01:44,493 --> 00:01:48,885
2020 CD3 looped around us for months before it left.

36
00:01:48,885 --> 00:01:53,869
And in 2024, a rock called 2024 PT5, about ten meters long,

37
00:01:53,869 --> 00:01:56,234
stayed for fifty-seven days.

38
00:01:56,734 --> 00:01:58,860
PT5 never completed a full loop.

39
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:01,849
But its colours matched samples of Moon rock.

40
00:02:01,849 --> 00:02:06,566
It may be a boulder blasted off the Moon long ago, briefly coming home.

41
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:10,467
So let's upgrade.

42
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:13,487
Imagine Earth's second moon isn't a boulder.

43
00:02:13,487 --> 00:02:15,134
It's a twin of the Moon.

44
00:02:15,134 --> 00:02:17,674
Same size, same grey rock, same mass.

45
00:02:18,174 --> 00:02:21,793
We'll put it on a circular orbit at half the Moon's distance,

46
00:02:21,793 --> 00:02:25,233
about one hundred and ninety-two thousand kilometers away.

47
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:27,487
We won't worry about how it got there.

48
00:02:27,487 --> 00:02:29,267
We'll just watch what happens.

49
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:31,454
Here's the first surprise.

50
00:02:31,454 --> 00:02:34,697
A closer moon has to move faster to stay in orbit.

51
00:02:34,697 --> 00:02:39,951
By our rough math, using Kepler's laws, the twin circles Earth in under ten days.

52
00:02:39,951 --> 00:02:43,000
That's almost three times faster than the Moon.

53
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:44,392
And it's close.

54
00:02:44,392 --> 00:02:48,790
Light from the twin would reach your eyes in about two thirds of a second.

55
00:02:48,790 --> 00:02:51,167
Moonlight takes about one and a quarter.

56
00:02:53,933 --> 00:02:56,774
On your first night, the twin rises in the east.

57
00:02:56,774 --> 00:02:57,602
And it's huge.

58
00:02:58,102 --> 00:03:01,485
At half the distance, it looks twice as wide as the Moon.

59
00:03:01,485 --> 00:03:03,088
That's four times the area.

60
00:03:03,088 --> 00:03:04,156
So when it's full,

61
00:03:04,156 --> 00:03:08,370
it should light the ground about four times as brightly as a full moon.

62
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:13,862
The two moons run at different speeds, so their phases drift in and out of step.

63
00:03:13,862 --> 00:03:17,481
Some nights, you get a full twin and a thin crescent Moon.

64
00:03:17,481 --> 00:03:21,599
Other nights, both are full, and the whole landscape glows silver.

65
00:03:21,599 --> 00:03:23,470
Truly dark nights become rare.

66
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:27,283
Like our Moon, the twin would soon be tidally locked,

67
00:03:27,283 --> 00:03:29,470
always showing Earth the same face.

68
00:03:29,470 --> 00:03:32,782
And its phases would come round every ten days or so,

69
00:03:32,782 --> 00:03:34,782
from new to full and back again.

70
00:03:35,282 --> 00:03:39,380
And when both moons are up, everything on the ground casts two shadows,

71
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:41,111
each pointing a different way.

72
00:03:43,867 --> 00:03:45,703
By morning, you'd notice the sea.

73
00:03:46,203 --> 00:03:51,153
Tides come from the difference in a moon's pull from one side of Earth to the other.

74
00:03:51,153 --> 00:03:53,805
And that difference fades fast with distance.

75
00:03:53,805 --> 00:03:55,926
It follows the cube of the distance.

76
00:03:55,926 --> 00:03:59,757
Halve the distance, and the tidal pull gets eight times stronger.

77
00:04:00,257 --> 00:04:04,807
So the twin pulls on our oceans about eight times harder than the Moon does.

78
00:04:04,807 --> 00:04:08,101
Add the Moon and the Sun on top, and by our rough math,

79
00:04:08,101 --> 00:04:13,610
the strongest tides would pull more than six times harder than today's biggest spring tides.

80
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:16,180
How high would the water climb?

81
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:20,188
Real tides depend on the shape of every coast and sea floor,

82
00:04:20,188 --> 00:04:21,925
so nobody can say exactly.

83
00:04:21,925 --> 00:04:27,403
But low, flat coastlines would flood and drain on a grand scale, every single day.

84
00:04:27,903 --> 00:04:32,845
And there'd be two tidal rhythms, one from each moon, beating against each other.

85
00:04:32,845 --> 00:04:34,615
On some days they'd stack up.

86
00:04:34,615 --> 00:04:36,812
On others, they'd partly cancel out.

87
00:04:39,567 --> 00:04:43,081
Halfway through our new life with two moons, a quick favour.

88
00:04:43,081 --> 00:04:45,892
If you're enjoying this, tap like and subscribe.

89
00:04:45,892 --> 00:04:47,532
It really helps the channel.

90
00:04:48,032 --> 00:04:51,445
Then, one afternoon, the twin slides in front of the Sun.

91
00:04:51,945 --> 00:04:56,856
Today, the Moon and the Sun look almost exactly the same size in our sky.

92
00:04:56,856 --> 00:05:00,355
That's why a total eclipse only just covers the Sun,

93
00:05:00,355 --> 00:05:03,719
and lets its glowing outer atmosphere, the corona,

94
00:05:03,719 --> 00:05:05,469
shine out around the edge.

95
00:05:05,969 --> 00:05:08,787
The twin looks about twice as wide as the Sun.

96
00:05:08,787 --> 00:05:10,932
So it covers it with room to spare.

97
00:05:10,932 --> 00:05:13,628
Its shadow on the ground would be far wider,

98
00:05:13,628 --> 00:05:17,488
but it would also hide the brightest, inner part of the corona.

99
00:05:17,988 --> 00:05:21,400
And because the twin laps Earth every ten days or so,

100
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:24,233
eclipses would probably come far more often.

101
00:05:24,233 --> 00:05:28,354
Sometimes, the twin would even pass in front of the Moon itself.

102
00:05:28,354 --> 00:05:29,900
A moon eclipsing a moon.

103
00:05:32,633 --> 00:05:34,034
Now let's fast-forward.

104
00:05:34,034 --> 00:05:36,226
Because tides don't just move water.

105
00:05:36,226 --> 00:05:37,566
They act like a brake.

106
00:05:38,066 --> 00:05:40,883
Earth spins faster than the Moon orbits,

107
00:05:40,883 --> 00:05:43,981
so it drags the tidal bulges slightly ahead.

108
00:05:43,981 --> 00:05:49,192
Friction slows Earth's spin, and the bulge's pull nudges the Moon outward.

109
00:05:49,192 --> 00:05:53,417
Today, the Moon drifts away by about 3.8 centimeters a year.

110
00:05:53,917 --> 00:05:56,388
Close in, that braking gets much stronger.

111
00:05:56,388 --> 00:05:59,329
Plugging the twin into the standard tidal formula,

112
00:05:59,329 --> 00:06:03,858
it would brake Earth's spin about sixty-four times harder than the Moon does.

113
00:06:04,358 --> 00:06:05,413
By our rough math,

114
00:06:05,413 --> 00:06:09,395
days would stretch by about twenty-five minutes every million years.

115
00:06:09,395 --> 00:06:12,968
With only our Moon, that takes more than sixty million years.

116
00:06:13,468 --> 00:06:19,134
And the twin itself would spiral outward about forty-five times faster than the Moon.

117
00:06:19,134 --> 00:06:20,934
Close to two meters a year.

118
00:06:20,934 --> 00:06:25,000
In effect, it steals Earth's spin, and uses it to climb away.

119
00:06:27,733 --> 00:06:29,408
And that's where the trouble starts.

120
00:06:29,907 --> 00:06:32,321
Two big moons don't ignore each other.

121
00:06:32,321 --> 00:06:34,480
Every time the twin laps the Moon,

122
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:38,164
the two pass within about two hundred thousand kilometers,

123
00:06:38,164 --> 00:06:39,562
and tug on each other.

124
00:06:39,562 --> 00:06:40,451
At that range,

125
00:06:40,451 --> 00:06:45,405
the Moon pulls on the twin with roughly one eightieth of the force Earth does.

126
00:06:45,905 --> 00:06:47,069
Each tug is small.

127
00:06:47,069 --> 00:06:49,203
But they repeat, again and again.

128
00:06:49,203 --> 00:06:53,664
And as the twin creeps outward, the gap between their orbits shrinks.

129
00:06:53,664 --> 00:06:58,060
Sooner or later, the tugs start to add up instead of cancelling out.

130
00:06:58,560 --> 00:06:59,104
In 1999,

131
00:06:59,104 --> 00:07:05,759
planetary scientist Robin Canup and her colleagues simulated young Earths with more than one moon.

132
00:07:05,759 --> 00:07:06,710
Their verdict:

133
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:09,494
systems like this are typically unstable.

134
00:07:09,494 --> 00:07:12,754
In most of their runs, it ended one of two ways.

135
00:07:12,754 --> 00:07:17,440
The moons crashed into each other, or the inner moon fell into Earth.

136
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:20,887
We can watch a slow version of this at Mars.

137
00:07:20,887 --> 00:07:26,043
Its inner moon, Phobos, creeps about two meters closer to Mars every century.

138
00:07:26,043 --> 00:07:30,597
In thirty to fifty million years, Mars is expected to pull it apart.

139
00:07:31,097 --> 00:07:35,828
Exactly when our twin would meet its end depends on details we've made up.

140
00:07:35,828 --> 00:07:40,559
But two big moons around one planet is probably not a forever arrangement.

141
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:42,552
And here's the twist.

142
00:07:42,552 --> 00:07:44,416
Earth may have tried this already.

143
00:07:44,916 --> 00:07:45,470
In 2011,

144
00:07:45,470 --> 00:07:49,974
planetary scientists Martin Jutzi and Erik Asphaug proposed that,

145
00:07:49,974 --> 00:07:53,577
soon after the Moon formed, Earth had a second moon.

146
00:07:53,577 --> 00:07:58,565
About a third as wide as the Moon, and around one thirtieth of its mass.

147
00:07:59,065 --> 00:08:03,053
In their model, it sat at a stable point in the Moon's own orbit,

148
00:08:03,053 --> 00:08:05,998
sharing its path, for tens of millions of years.

149
00:08:06,498 --> 00:08:09,893
Then it drifted out of place, and caught up with the Moon.

150
00:08:09,893 --> 00:08:11,765
Not with a bang, but at a crawl.

151
00:08:11,765 --> 00:08:14,106
About seven thousand kilometers an hour.

152
00:08:14,106 --> 00:08:17,501
For two moons, that's about as gentle as a collision gets.

153
00:08:18,001 --> 00:08:22,405
Instead of blasting a crater, it splattered across one side of the Moon,

154
00:08:22,405 --> 00:08:25,096
as a layer of rock tens of kilometers thick.

155
00:08:25,096 --> 00:08:29,744
And that might explain why the Moon's far side is so rugged and mountainous,

156
00:08:29,744 --> 00:08:32,068
and so different from the side we see.

157
00:08:32,568 --> 00:08:34,516
When the dust settled, Asphaug said,

158
00:08:34,516 --> 00:08:37,872
you had one moon that might have looked a lot like ours today.

159
00:08:38,372 --> 00:08:39,864
It's still a hypothesis.

160
00:08:39,864 --> 00:08:43,904
Other scientists think tides and heat can explain the difference.

161
00:08:43,904 --> 00:08:48,504
But the Moon might carry the remains of its twin on the side we never see.

162
00:08:49,367 --> 00:08:53,393
So a second big moon would bring brighter nights, double shadows,

163
00:08:53,393 --> 00:08:55,128
giant tides and longer days.

164
00:08:55,128 --> 00:08:57,296
Until, one day, the two moons meet.

165
00:08:57,796 --> 00:09:00,799
For now, our tiny second moon is about to leave us.

166
00:09:00,799 --> 00:09:04,332
If you could keep a second moon forever, would you want one?

167
00:09:04,332 --> 00:09:05,746
Tell me in the comments.
